1	//! A checker type converted to [`PropType`] from the checker's structured answers: type flags, a union's members, a
2	//! reference's type arguments, a tuple's element flags, an object's properties, index and call signatures, an alias's
3	//! symbol and arguments. The printed type (`typeToString`) names things for display and for the two facts the
4	//! structure does not state (see [`crate::printed`]); no shape is read from it.
5	//!
6	//! The conversion of a type is a function of the type, how deep it sits and which instantiations enclose it, never of
7	//! the order the checker was asked in, so the memo can be keyed by the checker's type handle (valid inside one
8	//! snapshot) without making the output depend on query order. A handle is also what tells one instantiation from
9	//! another while a type is being expanded (`Box<Box<string>>` is two instantiations of one declaration): only its
10	//! equality is read, inside one conversion, so the answer does not depend on the numbers. The recursion marker that
11	//! results is named by the declaration (the alias or interface) and holds no handle. The memo is keyed by the enclosing
12	//! instantiations a conversion actually asked about, not by all of them (see [`State::memo`]).
13	//!
14	//! Every descent into the parts of a type (members, properties, elements, holes) passes [`Converter::enter`], which
15	//! decides before anything is fetched or converted whether the type is an enclosing instantiation (a back-reference),
16	//! sits too deep, or is too wide.
17	//!
18	//! What stays a name instead of an expansion, the named boundary: an object type declared in a dependency
19	//! (`node_modules`) or an interface or class of the TypeScript lib (`Date`, `HTMLElement`, `Promise<T>`), with its type
20	//! arguments; a function type is always expanded, since its parameters are what a reader wants. The lib's type-level
21	//! utilities (`Partial`, `Pick`, `Omit`, `Record`) are evaluated, not named: their result is the type, unless it is
22	//! wider than the budget and what it applies to has a name (see [`Converter::over_budget`]). A dependency's union of
23	//! literals is expanded too, since its members are the values a control offers. What the caps cut is a typed
24	//! [`PropType::Truncated`], never the checker's text standing in as a name. The width of an object counts only the
25	//! members the project did not declare: its own are listed whole.
26	
27	use std::borrow::Borrow;
28	use std::future::Future;
29	use std::pin::Pin;
30	use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicU64, Ordering};
31	use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};
32	
33	use caliper_docgen_core::react_types::{html_element_for, html_element_from_type_arg, is_intrinsic_tag};
34	use caliper_docgen_core::types::semantic::{SemanticProp, degraded_type, unresolved_type};
35	use caliper_docgen_core::types::{
36	    BigIntValue, CallSignature, EnumMemberValue, FunctionParam, ObjectField, OpaqueReason, PropType,
37	    SignatureKind as Kind, TemplatePart, TruncationLimit, TupleElement, TupleElementKind, TupleLabel,
38	};
39	use rustc_hash::FxHashMap;
40	
41	use crate::asked::Answers;
42	use crate::declared::Declared;
43	use crate::printed::{Marker, SignatureMarkers, signature_markers, tuple_labels};
44	use crate::rpc::{
45	    CallError, CompactSymbol, IndexInfo, ObjectMembers, Rpc, SignatureInfo, SignatureKind, SymbolInfo, SymbolRef,
46	    TypeInfo, decode, flag,
47	};
48	use serde_json::value::RawValue;
49	
50	/// Nesting past which an object, union or intersection is cut with [`TruncationLimit::Depth`].
51	const MAX_DEPTH: u8 = 5;
52	/// The most properties an object type lists at the top (a prop's own type) and below it that the project did not declare
53	/// itself (see [`is_own`]): what it declared is never cut.
54	const MAX_FIELDS: [usize; 2] = [100, 40];
55	/// The most members a union or intersection lists at the top and below it.
56	const MAX_MEMBERS: [usize; 2] = [500, 120];
57	/// The most types one converted type holds, itself and every type inside it counted ([`past_size_budget`]): a type with
58	/// more is cut with [`TruncationLimit::Depth`] where it is converted, and the memo keeps the cut form. The caps above
59	/// bound each object, union and intersection, but not what the project declared itself (see [`is_own`]), so a style
60	/// system in the project's own source (Chakra's `SystemStyleObject`: about 700 properties, each a value or an object
61	/// of 130 conditions) expanded to 25 MB of JSON per prop and 42 GB of memory for one extract.
62	///
63	/// What is enforced is this count (18,724 types), not a byte size. It is chosen so that no type under 256 KiB of JSON
64	/// is ever cut: a type in the output is a JSON object with at least a `kind` (`{"kind":"any"}`, 14 bytes, is the
65	/// smallest), so a type of more than this many types is more than 256 KiB. On real projects a type averages about 40
66	/// bytes, so the cut falls near 0.75 MB of JSON: Chakra's largest prop type left whole is 732 KB. The largest prop
67	/// type of Mantine's core is 196 KB; Chakra's style props were 25 MB. The cut is reported as a depth truncation, since
68	/// a limit kind of its own would change the output schema.
69	const MAX_SIZE: usize = 256 * 1024 / MIN_TYPE_BYTES;
70	/// The fewest bytes one type takes in the JSON output: `{"kind":"any"}`.
71	const MIN_TYPE_BYTES: usize = 14;
72	// One type past the budget is more than 256 KiB.
73	const _: () = assert!((MAX_SIZE + 1) * MIN_TYPE_BYTES > 256 * 1024);
74	/// How many of React's tag names (see [`is_tag`]) a union has to list to be React's `ElementType` (`JSX.IntrinsicElements`
75	/// has over 170): the tag names are the one part of it that no declaration identifies.
76	const MIN_INTRINSIC_TAGS: usize = 100;
77	/// The lib's utility aliases that keep the properties of the type they are applied to, and so can stand for it.
78	const UTILITIES: [&str; 5] = ["Omit", "Pick", "Partial", "Required", "Readonly"];
79	
80	/// What a descent found at [`Converter::enter`].
81	enum Gate<T> {
82	    /// The type is not expanded: it is a back-reference, or a cap cut it.
83	    Stop(PropType),
84	    /// The parts of the type, and the context to convert them in.
85	    Open { parts: T, inner: Cx },
86	}
87	
88	type Converted<'a> = Pin<Box<dyn Future<Output = Result<PropType, CallError>> + Send + 'a>>;
89	
90	/// What an object's members are known as when its type is entered.
91	enum Listed {
92	    /// The checker was asked for them.
93	    Members(ObjectMembers),
94	    /// How many properties an earlier map over the same keys had, none of them the project's (see [`State::maps`]). It
95	    /// is only handed out where that is more than the budget allows, so the type is cut and no list is needed.
96	    Counted(usize),
97	}
98	
99	/// What [`Converter::over_budget`] reads of the members of an object it cuts.
100	struct Excess {
101	    /// The type has call or construct signatures.
102	    signatures: bool,
103	    /// How many of its properties the project did not declare.
104	    outside: usize,
105	    /// The properties the project declared.
106	    own: Vec<SymbolInfo>,
107	    indexes: Vec<IndexInfo>,
108	}
109	
110	impl Excess {
111	    fn of(members: ObjectMembers) -> Self {
112	        let ObjectMembers { calls, constructs, properties, indexes } = members;
113	        let (own, outside): (Vec<SymbolInfo>, Vec<SymbolInfo>) = properties.into_iter().partition(is_own);
114	        Excess { signatures: !calls.is_empty() || !constructs.is_empty(), outside: outside.len(), own, indexes }
115	    }
116	
117	    fn counted(outside: usize) -> Self {
118	        Excess { signatures: false, outside, own: Vec::new(), indexes: Vec::new() }
119	    }
120	}
121	
122	/// What a map is remembered by: its constraint, as the checker's type handle, and the symbol that declares it.
123	type MapKey = (u64, CompactSymbol);
124	
125	/// The key of a mapped type whose property count another map over the same keys can answer for: one no name stands for
126	/// (an alias's arguments, `Partial<A>`, are what its properties come from, and the key holds none), with no `as` clause
127	/// (which would rename the keys).
128	fn map_key(info: &TypeInfo, origin: Option<&Origin>) -> Option<MapKey> {
129	    if origin.is_some() || info.object_flags & flag::OBJECT_MAPPED == 0 || info.name_type.is_some() {
130	        return None;
131	    }
132	    Some((info.constraint_type?, info.symbol.clone()?))
133	}
134	
135	/// The number of properties of a map's listed members to remember, when the cut of another map over the same keys needs
136	/// no more of them: the project declared none, so none is listed, and the map has no signature and no index signature,
137	/// whose types are another map's own.
138	fn settled_count(members: &ObjectMembers) -> Option<usize> {
139	    let bare = members.calls.is_empty() && members.constructs.is_empty() && members.indexes.is_empty();
140	    (bare && members.properties.iter().all(|property| !is_own(property))).then_some(members.properties.len())
141	}
142	
143	/// Where a type sits: how deep, and which named instantiations (an alias or interface with its type arguments, as the
144	/// checker's type handle) enclose it, outermost first.
145	#[derive(Debug, Clone, Default)]
146	pub(crate) struct Cx {
147	    depth: u8,
148	    enclosing: Arc<Vec<u64>>,
149	    /// The conversion this context belongs to, which records which enclosing instantiations it asks about. `None` at
150	    /// the roots.
151	    frame: Option<Arc<Frame>>,
152	}
153	
154	/// What one conversion has asked of the instantiations enclosing it, kept to build its memo entry (see
155	/// [`State::memo`]).
156	#[derive(Debug)]
157	struct Frame {
158	    /// How many instantiations enclosed the type when its conversion began: those below this are the outer context, the
159	    /// rest were opened by the conversion itself.
160	    base: usize,
161	    asked: Mutex<Asked>,
162	}
163	
164	#[derive(Debug, Default)]
165	struct Asked {
166	    /// The handles this conversion's own gate compared against its enclosing instantiations.
167	    ids: Vec<u64>,
168	    /// The sets asked by the conversions of its parts, each with the instantiations this conversion had opened by then:
169	    /// those were answered inside it and do not depend on its outer context.
170	    parts: Vec<(Arc<[u64]>, Vec<u64>)>,
171	}
172	
173	impl Frame {
174	    fn new(base: usize) -> Self {
175	        Frame { base, asked: Mutex::default() }
176	    }
177	
178	    fn lock(&self) -> std::sync::MutexGuard<'_, Asked> {
179	        self.asked.lock().unwrap_or_else(std::sync::PoisonError::into_inner)
180	    }
181	
182	    fn ask(&self, id: u64) {
183	        self.lock().ids.push(id);
184	    }
185	
186	    /// A part of this conversion was converted (or recalled) under `enclosing`, having asked about `asked`.
187	    fn absorb(&self, asked: &Arc<[u64]>, enclosing: &[u64]) {
188	        if !asked.is_empty() {
189	            self.lock().parts.push((asked.clone(), enclosing.get(self.base..).unwrap_or_default().to_vec()));
190	        }
191	    }
192	
193	    /// Every handle the conversion asked about whose answer came from outside it: sorted, once each.
194	    fn finish(&self) -> Arc<[u64]> {
195	        let asked = self.lock();
196	        let mut ids = asked.ids.clone();
197	        for (part, opened) in &asked.parts {
198	            ids.extend(part.iter().copied().filter(|id| !opened.contains(id)));
199	        }
200	        ids.sort_unstable();
201	        ids.dedup();
202	        ids.into()
203	    }
204	}
205	
206	impl Cx {
207	    /// The context of what a name carries, its type arguments: one level down. Each argument is converted on its own
208	    /// and passes [`Converter::enter`] if it has parts.
209	    fn argument(&self) -> Cx {
210	        Cx { depth: self.depth.saturating_add(1), enclosing: self.enclosing.clone(), frame: self.frame.clone() }
211	    }
212	
213	    /// The context of the parts of a type that [`Converter::enter`] has admitted. Only a named instance is kept, since
214	    /// only a name can be pointed back to.
215	    fn below(&self, instance: Option<u64>) -> Cx {
216	        let mut enclosing = self.enclosing.clone();
217	        if let Some(id) = instance
218	            && !enclosing.contains(&id)
219	        {
220	            let mut grown = (*enclosing).clone();
221	            grown.push(id);
222	            enclosing = Arc::new(grown);
223	        }
224	        Cx { depth: self.depth.saturating_add(1), enclosing, frame: self.frame.clone() }
225	    }
226	
227	    /// Whether `id` is an instantiation being expanded around the type. The conversion in progress is recorded to have
228	    /// asked, whichever the answer.
229	    fn encloses(&self, id: u64) -> bool {
230	        if let Some(frame) = &self.frame {
231	            frame.ask(id);
232	        }
233	        self.enclosing.contains(&id)
234	    }
235	
236	    fn limit<const N: usize>(&self, limits: [usize; N]) -> usize {
237	        limits[usize::from(self.depth > 0).min(N - 1)]
238	    }
239	}
240	
241	/// What declares a type: the alias it was written through, else its interface or class.
242	#[derive(Debug, Clone)]
243	struct Origin {
244	    name: String,
245	    /// The declaring file as the checker keys it (lower-cased path).
246	    file: String,
247	    alias: bool,
248	}
249	
250	impl Origin {
251	    fn of(symbol: &SymbolInfo, alias: bool) -> Self {
252	        let declaration = symbol.declarations.first().map(String::as_str);
253	        Origin {
254	            name: symbol.name.clone(),
255	            file: declaration.map(|declaration| declared_in(declaration).to_string()).unwrap_or_default(),
256	            alias,
257	        }
258	    }
259	
260	    fn in_dependency(&self) -> bool {
261	        file_in_dependency(&self.file)
262	    }
263	
264	    fn in_lib(&self) -> bool {
265	        file_in_lib(&self.file)
266	    }
267	
268	    fn in_react(&self) -> bool {
269	        self.file.contains("/@types/react/")
270	    }
271	
272	    /// Whether the name stands for the type: declared in a dependency, or an interface or class of the lib. An alias
273	    /// of the lib is a type-level function (`Partial<T>`), and its result is the type.
274	    fn is_boundary(&self) -> bool {
275	        // The lib may itself sit under `node_modules` (`typescript/lib`, `@tsrs/<platform>`).
276	        if self.in_lib() { !self.alias } else { self.in_dependency() }
277	    }
278	
279	    /// The React type that PropType holds as a terminal, found by the declaration and not by how it prints.
280	    /// `ReactElement` and `JSX.Element` are not `ReactNode`, which also admits strings, numbers and `null`: they keep
281	    /// their names ([`Self::name`]).
282	    fn react_terminal(&self) -> Option<PropType> {
283	        if !self.in_react() {
284	            return None;
285	        }
286	        match (self.name.as_str(), self.alias) {
287	            ("ReactNode", true) => Some(PropType::ReactNode),
288	            ("ElementType", true) => Some(PropType::ElementType),
289	            ("CSSProperties", false) => Some(PropType::CssProperties),
290	            _ => None,
291	        }
292	    }
293	
294	    /// The name a reference to the type is given. React's `JSX.Element` is named through its namespace: the checker's
295	    /// `Element` is also the DOM's (which `@types/react` declares empty in `global.d.ts` for a program without it).
296	    fn name(&self) -> String {
297	        if self.in_react() && !self.alias && self.name == "Element" && !self.file.ends_with("/global.d.ts") {
298	            return "JSX.Element".to_string();
299	        }
300	        self.name.clone()
301	    }
302	}
303	
304	/// Whether a union member is a tag of React's `JSX.IntrinsicElements`: a string literal (not an enum member) whose value
305	/// that table lists. A literal the checker gave no value for is not one, so it is kept beside the terminal.
306	fn is_tag(member: &TypeInfo) -> bool {
307	    member.flags & flag::STRING_LITERAL != 0
308	        && member.flags & flag::ENUM_LITERAL == 0
309	        && member.value.as_ref().and_then(|value| value.as_str()).is_some_and(is_intrinsic_tag)
310	}
311	
312	/// Whether the checker states the base types of this type in its own terms: the declaration of a class or interface
313	/// does. An instantiation of a generic one (`Box<string>`, only a reference) does not state them in terms of its
314	/// arguments, so a traversal that keeps the arguments cannot list what it inherits; one that wants only names reads the
315	/// generic type instead ([`Converter::named_bases`]).
316	fn states_bases(info: &TypeInfo) -> bool {
317	    info.object_flags & (flag::OBJECT_CLASS | flag::OBJECT_INTERFACE) != 0
318	}
319	
320	/// Where the base types of a type are to be asked, from what the checker says of the type itself.
321	#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
322	enum BasesOf {
323	    /// A class or interface declaration: the checker states them ([`states_bases`]).
324	    Itself,
325	    /// An instantiation of a generic type: its generic target states them.
326	    Generic,
327	    /// An object type no class or interface declares (a type literal, a mapped type): it has none.
328	    None,
329	    /// A union, a type parameter or any other type the checker was not asked about: whether it has bases is not known.
330	    Unknown,
331	}
332	
333	impl BasesOf {
334	    fn of(info: &TypeInfo) -> Self {
335	        if states_bases(info) {
336	            Self::Itself
337	        } else if info.object_flags & flag::OBJECT_REFERENCE != 0 {
338	            Self::Generic
339	        } else if info.flags & flag::OBJECT != 0 {
340	            Self::None
341	        } else {
342	            Self::Unknown
343	        }
344	    }
345	}
346	
347	fn file_in_dependency(file: &str) -> bool {
348	    file.contains("/node_modules/")
349	}
350	
351	fn file_in_lib(file: &str) -> bool {
352	    let name = file.rsplit('/').next().unwrap_or("");
353	    name.starts_with("lib.") && name.ends_with(".d.ts")
354	}
355	
356	/// Whether a property is the project's own: its first declaration is in a file of the project, not a dependency or the
357	/// lib. A property with no declaration (a mapped type's synthetic one) is not, so a wide mapped type is still cut.
358	fn is_own(property: &SymbolInfo) -> bool {
359	    property.declarations.first().is_some_and(|declaration| {
360	        let file = declared_in(declaration);
361	        !file_in_dependency(file) && !file_in_lib(file)
362	    })
363	}
364	
365	/// `declarations` entries read `"<node index>.<kind>.<path>"`: the path is lower-cased, as the checker keys files.
366	pub(crate) fn declared_in(declaration: &str) -> &str {
367	    declaration.splitn(3, '.').nth(2).unwrap_or("")
368	}
369	
370	/// Whether `converted` holds more than `budget` types, itself and every type inside it counted. Counted on the tree,
371	/// not by serializing it, and only as far as the budget: the walk stops at the first type past it.
372	fn past_size_budget(converted: &PropType, budget: usize) -> bool {
373	    past_budget(vec![converted], 0, budget)
374	}
375	
376	/// Whether an object of `fields` holds more than `budget` types, as [`past_size_budget`] counts them.
377	fn fields_past_size_budget(fields: &[ObjectField], budget: usize) -> bool {
378	    let mut pending = Vec::new();
379	    push_fields(fields, &mut pending);
380	    past_budget(pending, 1, budget)
381	}
382	
383	/// Whether `counted` types, and those in `pending` with every type inside them, are more than `budget`.
384	fn past_budget(mut pending: Vec<&PropType>, counted: usize, budget: usize) -> bool {
385	    let mut count = counted;
386	    while let Some(ty) = pending.pop() {
387	        count += 1;
388	        if count > budget {
389	            return true;
390	        }
391	        push_inner(ty, &mut pending);
392	    }
393	    false
394	}
395	
396	/// Pushes every type directly inside `ty` onto `pending`: fields and index keys, signatures, arguments, tuple elements
397	/// and template holes.
398	fn push_inner<'a>(ty: &'a PropType, pending: &mut Vec<&'a PropType>) {
399	    match ty {
400	        PropType::String
401	        | PropType::Number
402	        | PropType::Boolean
403	        | PropType::Null
404	        | PropType::Undefined
405	        | PropType::Any
406	        | PropType::Never
407	        | PropType::Unknown
408	        | PropType::Void
409	        | PropType::BigInt
410	        | PropType::StringLiteral(_)
411	        | PropType::NumberLiteral(_)
412	        | PropType::BoolLiteral(_)
413	        | PropType::BigIntLiteral(_)
414	        | PropType::UniqueSymbol { .. }
415	        | PropType::EnumMember { .. }
416	        | PropType::Truncated { .. }
417	        | PropType::ReactNode
418	        | PropType::CssProperties
419	        | PropType::Ref { .. }
420	        | PropType::ElementType
421	        | PropType::HtmlAttributes { .. }
422	        | PropType::LiteralUnion { .. }
423	        | PropType::SxProps
424	        | PropType::Opaque(_) => {}
425	        PropType::TemplateLiteral(parts) => pending.extend(parts.iter().filter_map(|part| match part {
426	            TemplatePart::Text(_) => None,
427	            TemplatePart::Type(ty) => Some(ty),
428	        })),
429	        PropType::Union(members)
430	        | PropType::Intersection(members)
431	        | PropType::Named { args: members, .. }
432	        | PropType::Recursive { args: members, .. } => pending.extend(members),
433	        PropType::Array(inner) | PropType::Readonly(inner) => pending.push(inner),
434	        PropType::Tuple(elements) => pending.extend(elements.iter().map(|element| &element.element_type)),
435	        PropType::Object(fields) => push_fields(fields, pending),
436	        PropType::Callable { signatures, fields } => {
437	            for signature in signatures {
438	                pending.extend(signature.params.iter().map(|param| &param.param_type));
439	                pending.push(&signature.return_type);
440	            }
441	            push_fields(fields, pending);
442	        }
443	        PropType::Function { params, return_type, .. } => {
444	            pending.extend(params.iter().map(|param| &param.param_type));
445	            pending.push(return_type);
446	        }
447	    }
448	}
449	
450	/// The types of `fields` for [`past_size_budget`] and [`fingerprint`]: each field's, and an index signature's key type.
451	fn push_fields<'a>(fields: &'a [ObjectField], pending: &mut Vec<&'a PropType>) {
452	    for field in fields {
453	        pending.push(&field.prop_type);
454	        if let Some(key) = &field.index_key {
455	            pending.push(key);
456	        }
457	    }
458	}
459	
460	/// A hash of everything in `ty` that tells most types apart: each type's kind, how many types it holds directly, and its
461	/// names, texts and values. What it leaves out (a field's flags, a tuple's labels) only puts more types in one bucket of
462	/// [`Trees`], which tells them apart by equality. `None` for a type holding a number that equality cannot vouch for:
463	/// NaN equals nothing, and `-0` equals `0` but is written apart.
464	fn fingerprint(ty: &PropType) -> Option<u64> {
465	    use std::hash::{Hash, Hasher};
466	    let mut hasher = rustc_hash::FxHasher::default();
467	    let mut pending = vec![ty];
468	    while let Some(ty) = pending.pop() {
469	        std::mem::discriminant(ty).hash(&mut hasher);
470	        match ty {
471	            PropType::String
472	            | PropType::Number
473	            | PropType::Boolean
474	            | PropType::Null
475	            | PropType::Undefined
476	            | PropType::Any
477	            | PropType::Never
478	            | PropType::Unknown
479	            | PropType::Void
480	            | PropType::BigInt
481	            | PropType::ReactNode
482	            | PropType::CssProperties
483	            | PropType::Ref { .. }
484	            | PropType::ElementType
485	            | PropType::HtmlAttributes { .. }
486	            | PropType::LiteralUnion { .. }
487	            | PropType::SxProps
488	            | PropType::Opaque(_)
489	            | PropType::TemplateLiteral(_)
490	            | PropType::Union(_)
491	            | PropType::Intersection(_)
492	            | PropType::Array(_)
493	            | PropType::Readonly(_)
494	            | PropType::Tuple(_)
495	            | PropType::Function { .. } => {}
496	            PropType::StringLiteral(text) => text.hash(&mut hasher),
497	            PropType::UniqueSymbol { name } => name.hash(&mut hasher),
498	            PropType::NumberLiteral(number) => vouched(*number)?.hash(&mut hasher),
499	            PropType::BoolLiteral(value) => value.hash(&mut hasher),
500	            PropType::BigIntLiteral(value) => value.as_str().hash(&mut hasher),
501	            PropType::EnumMember { enum_name, member, value } => {
502	                enum_name.hash(&mut hasher);
503	                member.hash(&mut hasher);
504	                match value {
505	                    EnumMemberValue::String(text) => text.hash(&mut hasher),
506	                    EnumMemberValue::Number(number) => vouched(*number)?.hash(&mut hasher),
507	                }
508	            }
509	            PropType::Truncated { text, .. } => text.hash(&mut hasher),
510	            PropType::Named { name, .. } | PropType::Recursive { name, .. } => name.hash(&mut hasher),
511	            PropType::Object(fields) | PropType::Callable { fields, .. } => {
512	                fields.iter().for_each(|field| field.name.hash(&mut hasher));
513	            }
514	        }
515	        let before = pending.len();
516	        push_inner(ty, &mut pending);
517	        (pending.len() - before).hash(&mut hasher);
518	    }
519	    Some(hasher.finish())
520	}
521	
522	/// The bits of `number` when equality tells it from every number written otherwise: not NaN, and not `-0`.
523	fn vouched(number: f64) -> Option<u64> {
524	    (!number.is_nan() && !(number == 0.0 && number.is_sign_negative())).then(|| number.to_bits())
525	}
526	
527	/// The converted types of a session's memos, each held once however many entries of however many lanes convert to it:
528	/// Chakra's 3.6 million memo types are about 7,500 distinct ones. A tree is shared only with one equal to it, so what
529	/// an entry recalls is what was converted.
530	#[derive(Default)]
531	pub(crate) struct Trees {
532	    by_fingerprint: Mutex<FxHashMap<u64, Vec<Arc<PropType>>>>,
533	}
534	
535	impl Trees {
536	    /// The tree equal to `converted` held before, or a copy of it, held from now on. A copy of its own, not held, for a
537	    /// tree that cannot be [fingerprinted](fingerprint).
538	    fn share(&self, converted: &PropType) -> Arc<PropType> {
539	        let Some(fingerprint) = fingerprint(converted) else { return Arc::new(converted.clone()) };
540	        let mut trees = self.by_fingerprint.lock().unwrap_or_else(std::sync::PoisonError::into_inner);
541	        let bucket = trees.entry(fingerprint).or_default();
542	        if let Some(held) = bucket.iter().find(|held| ***held == *converted) {
543	            return held.clone();
544	        }
545	        let held = Arc::new(converted.clone());
546	        bucket.push(held.clone());
547	        held
548	    }
549	}
550	
551	/// A converted type, with the enclosing instantiations its conversion depended on.
552	#[derive(Debug)]
553	struct Entry {
554	    /// Every handle the conversion compared against the instantiations enclosing it, sorted.
555	    asked: Arc<[u64]>,
556	    /// Those of `asked` that did enclose it.
557	    enclosed_by: Vec<u64>,
558	    /// Shared with every other entry of the session that converted to an equal type ([`Trees`]).
559	    converted: Arc<PropType>,
560	}
561	
562	impl Entry {
563	    /// Whether the conversion would go the same way inside `enclosing`: among the handles it asked about, the ones
564	    /// that enclose the type there are exactly `enclosed_by`.
565	    fn holds_in(&self, enclosing: &[u64]) -> bool {
566	        self.enclosed_by.iter().all(|id| enclosing.contains(id))
567	            && enclosing.iter().all(|id| self.enclosed_by.contains(id) || self.asked.binary_search(id).is_err())
568	    }
569	}
570	
571	type Memo = Mutex<FxHashMap<(u64, u8), Vec<Arc<Entry>>>>;
572	
573	/// The props answered so far, by the hash of their [`PropKey`] and then the key itself, owned.
574	type KeptProps = Mutex<FxHashMap<u64, Vec<(KeptKey, Arc<SemanticProp>)>>>;
575	
576	/// Everything a component's prop is made of ([`crate::component::props_of_type`]): its property's name, optionality and
577	/// declarations, the symbol it is a member of (its JSDoc's key when it has no declaration: the symbol itself), its type's
578	/// handle, and which of its JSDoc the component asks for. Two props of one snapshot with equal keys are the same prop: the
579	/// type is converted from the handle alone at the top ([`Converter::convert_roots`]), and where it is declared and its
580	/// JSDoc are read from the declarations, the parent and the symbol alone ([`crate::declared`]). Chakra's components
581	/// share a thousand style props, each a union converted from the same handle, so a component after the first finds
582	/// them here and neither converts, copies nor interns them again.
583	///
584	/// The key borrows what it is made of from the property: most props are found kept, and only one that is not is copied,
585	/// into the [`KeptKey`] it is kept by.
586	#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
587	pub(crate) struct PropKey<'a> {
588	    hash: u64,
589	    name: &'a str,
590	    optional: bool,
591	    type_id: u64,
592	    declarations: &'a [String],
593	    parent: Option<&'a CompactSymbol>,
594	    /// The symbol's reference, only for a property with no declaration.
595	    symbol: Option<&'a str>,
596	    description: bool,
597	    tags: bool,
598	}
599	
600	impl<'a> PropKey<'a> {
601	    pub(crate) fn of(property: &'a SymbolInfo, prop_type: &TypeInfo, description: bool, tags: bool) -> Self {
602	        use std::hash::{Hash, Hasher};
603	        let optional = property.flags & flag::SYMBOL_OPTIONAL != 0;
604	        let symbol = property.declarations.is_empty().then(|| property.reference.text());
605	        let parent = property.parent.as_ref();
606	        let mut hasher = rustc_hash::FxHasher::default();
607	        (&property.name, optional, prop_type.id, &property.declarations, parent, symbol, description, tags)
608	            .hash(&mut hasher);
609	        PropKey {
610	            hash: hasher.finish(),
611	            name: &property.name,
612	            optional,
613	            type_id: prop_type.id,
614	            declarations: &property.declarations,
615	            parent,
616	            symbol,
617	            description,
618	            tags,
619	        }
620	    }
621	
622	    /// Whether `kept` was made from what this key names.
623	    fn names(&self, kept: &KeptKey) -> bool {
624	        self.name == kept.name
625	            && self.optional == kept.optional
626	            && self.type_id == kept.type_id
627	            && self.declarations == kept.declarations.as_slice()
628	            && self.parent == kept.parent.as_ref()
629	            && self.symbol == kept.symbol.as_deref()
630	            && self.description == kept.description
631	            && self.tags == kept.tags
632	    }
633	
634	    fn to_kept(self) -> KeptKey {
635	        KeptKey {
636	            name: self.name.to_string(),
637	            optional: self.optional,
638	            type_id: self.type_id,
639	            declarations: self.declarations.to_vec(),
640	            parent: self.parent.cloned(),
641	            symbol: self.symbol.map(str::to_string),
642	            description: self.description,
643	            tags: self.tags,
644	        }
645	    }
646	}
647	
648	/// A [`PropKey`] that owns what it is made of, for a prop kept for the components after its own.
649	#[derive(Debug)]
650	struct KeptKey {
651	    name: String,
652	    optional: bool,
653	    type_id: u64,
654	    declarations: Vec<String>,
655	    parent: Option<CompactSymbol>,
656	    symbol: Option<String>,
657	    description: bool,
658	    tags: bool,
659	}
660	
661	/// Which parts of a type [`Converter::parts`] asks for.
662	#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Hash)]
663	enum Parts {
664	    /// A union's, intersection's or template literal's members (`getTypesOfType`).
665	    Members,
666	    /// A reference's type arguments (`getTypeArguments`), the `this` type the checker appends included.
667	    Arguments,
668	}
669	
670	/// Which of an object type's properties [`Converter::fields`] lists: all of them, or only the project's own (see
671	/// [`Converter::over_budget`]). With the type's handle it names the same symbols every time, since an object type's
672	/// properties are the checker's answer about the type.
673	#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Hash)]
674	enum Listing {
675	    All,
676	    Own,
677	}
678	
679	/// The type arguments a reference names, without the `this` type the checker appends to a class's or interface's.
680	fn without_this(arguments: &[TypeInfo]) -> &[TypeInfo] {
681	    let named = arguments.iter().rposition(|argument| !argument.is_this_type).map_or(0, |last| last + 1);
682	    arguments.get(..named).unwrap_or_default()
683	}
684	
685	#[derive(Default)]
686	struct State {
687	    /// Conversions by (type handle, depth), each entry with the enclosing instantiations it depended on. The handles
688	    /// are valid inside this snapshot only.
689	    ///
690	    /// Why an entry may be reused where [`Entry::holds_in`] says. A conversion reads its context in two ways only: the
691	    /// depth, which is in the key, and [`Cx::encloses`], the question whether a handle is an instantiation being
692	    /// expanded around the type. Everything else it reads is the checker's answer about the type, which does not change
693	    /// inside a snapshot. So a conversion is a deterministic walk whose every step is fixed by the type, the depth and
694	    /// the answers to those questions, in the order they are asked. Run it again where each handle it asked about has
695	    /// the same answer, and it asks the same questions in the same order and returns the same type. A handle that the
696	    /// conversion opened itself is settled inside it (it was found not to enclose the type at its own gate, which is
697	    /// recorded), so only the handles asked of the outer context are kept. A type in no cycle with its context asks
698	    /// nothing that encloses it and holds everywhere; one in a cycle holds where the same part of the cycle encloses
699	    /// it. Entries that hold in a context all agree, so which of them is found first, and so the order in which
700	    /// types were first reached, cannot show in the output.

